# Security and privacy rules

This app stores users' sensitive personal documents — receipts, tax forms,
IDs, medical records, contracts. Treat every decision as if it will be
security-reviewed before it ships, because it will be.

- Every table containing user data must have Row Level Security enabled with
  a policy scoped to `auth.uid()` as part of the same migration that creates
  it — never "add RLS later."
- Never log document content, email bodies, file contents, or full file
  names with personally identifying info. Logs may contain IDs, status
  codes, byte sizes, and timestamps only.
- Any long-lived credential (OAuth refresh token, API key stored per-user)
  is encrypted at the database-storage level and decrypted only inside the
  worker process at the moment of use. It is never returned in an API
  response and never sent to the client, under any circumstance, including
  for debugging.
- Session tokens live in httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies. Never
  `localStorage` or `sessionStorage` for anything auth-related — this
  applies even in scaffolding or "temporary" code, because temporary code
  ships.
- Re-validate file type, size, and the user's storage/plan quota on the
  server for every upload, even though the client also checks. Client-side
  checks are UX only, never the security boundary.
- Every webhook handler (Stripe, inbound email) verifies its signature
  against the raw request body before reading or acting on anything in the
  payload. Reject unsigned or invalid-signature requests with no further
  processing.
- Treat every uploaded or received file as hostile input: enforce a hard
  size limit, detect file type from the file's actual bytes (magic-byte
  sniffing), not the extension or declared Content-Type, and never execute,
  eval, or render as HTML anything extracted from a file or email.
- Rate-limit every public-facing endpoint, especially auth routes and the
  inbound-email webhook.
- If a request in a phase prompt would weaken any rule above, stop and flag
  it back to the founder instead of implementing it as asked.
